1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,848
Even if you've never held a controller,
Link feels familiar.

2
00:00:04,198 --> 00:00:08,042
A kid leaves home, faces impossible
problems, and keeps going.

3
00:00:08,392 --> 00:00:10,362
It's one of the oldest stories we have.

4
00:00:11,262 --> 00:00:17,196
In 1949, the mythologist Joseph Campbell
published The Hero with a Thousand Faces.

5
00:00:17,546 --> 00:00:22,481
Reading myths from all over the world, he
kept meeting the same figure: one hero,

6
00:00:22,481 --> 00:00:23,943
wearing different faces.

7
00:00:24,293 --> 00:00:26,936
Leave home. Face trials. Return changed.

8
00:00:27,286 --> 00:00:30,551
Carl Jung called recurring figures like
this archetypes.

9
00:00:30,901 --> 00:00:35,225
Campbell's real discovery wasn't a
formula. It was the recurrence itself.

10
00:00:35,575 --> 00:00:39,653
So why does every culture keep inventing
the same hero? Hold that thought.

11
00:00:40,553 --> 00:00:45,966
Take Link in Ocarina of Time, from 1998:
an ordinary boy from the forest,

12
00:00:45,966 --> 00:00:49,599
until the Great Deku Tree calls him to
adventure.

13
00:00:49,949 --> 00:00:51,422
He draws the Master Sword,

14
00:00:51,422 --> 00:00:54,766
and wakes seven years later in a Hyrule
fallen to darkness.

15
00:00:55,116 --> 00:00:57,647
Like every player, he fails, and tries
again.

16
00:00:57,997 --> 00:01:01,438
And across the series, the hero keeps
returning in new forms.

17
00:01:02,338 --> 00:01:05,966
Nearly four thousand years ago, Gilgamesh
tried to defeat death.

18
00:01:06,316 --> 00:01:08,928
His solution failed, and he came home
changed.

19
00:01:09,278 --> 00:01:13,199
Heracles faced his labours. When two
didn't count, he did two more.

20
00:01:13,549 --> 00:01:15,558
Odysseus spent ten years getting home.

21
00:01:15,908 --> 00:01:20,204
And in Arthurian legend, only the true
king could draw the sword from the stone.

22
00:01:20,554 --> 00:01:21,485
Sound familiar?

23
00:01:22,385 --> 00:01:23,876
The faces keep changing.

24
00:01:25,576 --> 00:01:30,386
Frodo carries the burden. Sam carries
Frodo. And Aragorn returns as king.

25
00:01:32,086 --> 00:01:33,787
Master Chief finishes the fight.

26
00:01:35,487 --> 00:01:36,736
Luke answers the call.

27
00:01:38,436 --> 00:01:41,198
Major Kusanagi asks what makes her
herself.

28
00:01:42,898 --> 00:01:44,425
Guts struggles against fate.

29
00:01:46,125 --> 00:01:47,963
Goku trains past every limit.

30
00:01:49,663 --> 00:01:51,931
Naruto never goes back on his word.

31
00:01:53,631 --> 00:01:58,325
Nausicaä studies the poisoned forest, and
finds the real problem is the soil.

32
00:02:00,025 --> 00:02:01,002
Neo chooses.

33
00:02:02,702 --> 00:02:06,806
And K learns he isn't the chosen one, and
does the heroic thing anyway.

34
00:02:06,806 --> 00:02:09,060
He brings Deckard home to his daughter.

35
00:02:09,960 --> 00:02:11,912
No hero solves problems alone.

36
00:02:12,712 --> 00:02:17,618
The mentor passes on knowledge: Merlin,
Gandalf, Krishna, the Great Deku Tree.

37
00:02:18,418 --> 00:02:22,861
The shadow is the problem given a face:
Ganondorf, Vader, Ravana.

38
00:02:23,661 --> 00:02:29,026
The trickster breaks the rules and exposes
mistakes: Loki, Anansi, Sun Wukong.

39
00:02:29,826 --> 00:02:33,391
And the wise guide lights the way:
Princess Zelda, or Guanyin.

40
00:02:34,291 --> 00:02:36,472
So why does the same hero keep appearing?

41
00:02:36,822 --> 00:02:40,994
Campbell found the pattern. Karl Popper
and David Deutsch help explain it.

42
00:02:41,344 --> 00:02:45,865
Popper argued that all life is problem
solving. We guess, we make mistakes,

43
00:02:45,865 --> 00:02:47,071
and we correct them.

44
00:02:47,421 --> 00:02:52,304
Deutsch went further. Every person, he
argues, is a universal explainer: able,

45
00:02:52,304 --> 00:02:56,061
in principle, to understand anything that
can be understood.

46
00:02:56,411 --> 00:03:00,812
He put it in two lines. Problems are
inevitable. Problems are soluble.

47
00:03:01,162 --> 00:03:04,789
A world full of problem-solvers keeps
telling stories about one.

48
00:03:05,139 --> 00:03:07,492
That's why the thousand faces feel like
one.

49
00:03:07,842 --> 00:03:12,228
The hero is what greatness looks like
against a problem: wrong sometimes,

50
00:03:12,228 --> 00:03:14,451
tested always, and never out of hope.

51
00:03:15,351 --> 00:03:18,101
Your next problem is already waiting. Go
meet it.

52
00:03:18,451 --> 00:03:19,705
And subscribe for more.
